    <body>
      <sec id="article-114238.s1" sec-type="Definition/Introduction">
        <title>Definition/Introduction</title>
        <p content-type="pubmed-excerpt">FODMAP is an acronym for fermentable oligo-, di-, and monosaccharides, and polyols&#x02014;short-chain carbohydrates that are poorly absorbed in the small intestine and subsequently fermented by colonic bacteria. These carbohydrate groups are associated with the induction or worsening of functional gastrointestinal symptoms, including bloating, abdominal pain, cramping, and diarrhea. Their osmotic effects increase luminal water content, while bacterial fermentation produces gases and short-chain fatty acids, leading to intestinal distension. In individuals with visceral hypersensitivity, this distension contributes to the characteristic symptoms of irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) and other functional bowel disorders.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-114238.r1">[1]</xref>&#x000a0;Common dietary sources of FODMAPs include stone fruits, legumes, lactose-containing foods, wheat and other fructan-containing grains, vegetables such as onions and garlic, and foods containing polyol sweeteners.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-114238.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-114238.r3">[3]</xref></p>
        <p>Dietary therapy has become central to the management of functional gastrointestinal disorders, particularly IBS, because pharmacologic options often provide incomplete symptom relief or are associated with adverse effects. Among dietary interventions, the low FODMAP diet has emerged as a targeted, evidence-based approach for symptom management in selected patients.&#x000a0;The primary indication is IBS, for which the diet serves as a second-line strategy after unsuccessful lifestyle and dietary interventions. The diet is based on the framework developed by Monash University (Melbourne, Australia), which provides detailed guidance on FODMAP content in foods, structured phases of the diet, and supports dietitian-led implementation.</p>
        <p>The low FODMAP diet reduces exposure to fermentable substrates, thereby mitigating symptom triggers. Importantly, the low FODMAP diet is not a lifelong restrictive eating pattern or a general &#x0201c;healthy diet,&#x0201d; but rather designed as a structured therapeutic intervention implemented in 3 phases: an initial elimination phase, a systematic reintroduction phase, and a personalization phase to identify individual tolerance thresholds and maximize dietary variety. The ultimate goal is to liberalize the diet to include well-tolerated foods with some FODMAP content while maintaining symptom control. Clinical trials suggest that up to three-quarters of patients with IBS may experience symptomatic improvement within 6 weeks of initiation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-114238.r4">[4]</xref></p>
        <p>Given its complexity and potential risks when misused, clinicians should oversee the low FODMAP diet, with a registered dietitian providing optimal implementation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-114238.r5">[5]</xref>&#x000a0;Unsupervised or prolonged restriction may lead to unnecessary dietary limitations, nutrient inadequacy, adverse effects on the gut microbiota, and increased risk of disordered eating behaviors. Accordingly, careful patient selection, clear counseling on expectations and duration, and coordinated interprofessional care are essential for safe and effective implementation. This topic highlights the low FODMAP diet in clinical practice, focusing on evidence-based indications, mechanistic rationale, clinical significance, guideline recommendations, structured implementation, potential risks, and the role of interprofessional care in optimizing patient outcomes.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-114238.s2" sec-type="Issues of Concern">
        <title>Issues of Concern</title>
        <p>
<bold>Medical Indications</bold>
</p>
        <p>The primary evidence-based indication for a low FODMAP diet is IBS, including diarrhea-predominant (IBS-D), constipation-predominant (IBS-C), and mixed-subtype (IBS-M) presentations. It serves as second-line dietary therapy for patients who do not achieve adequate symptom control with standard lifestyle and nutritional recommendations, including eating small, regular meals and avoiding excess insoluble fiber, fatty foods, alcohol, caffeine, and known "trigger" foods.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-114238.r2">[2]</xref>&#x000a0;Other clinical contexts in which a low FODMAP diet may play a supportive role include functional bloating or abdominal distension, inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) in remission with ongoing IBS-like symptoms, and as an adjunctive measure to control symptoms in patients with small intestinal bacterial overgrowth (SIBO).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-114238.r6">[6]</xref></p>
        <p>Certain patient populations are not appropriate candidates for a low FODMAP diet. These include individuals with active IBD, celiac disease (who require gluten exclusion, not FODMAP restriction), food allergies or intolerances unrelated to fermentable carbohydrates, and those pursuing the diet for weight loss or metabolic optimization rather than symptom management. Clinicians should screen for eating disorders before initiating a low FODMAP diet, as the diet may reinforce overly restrictive eating patterns and exacerbate co-existing eating disorders.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-114238.r7">[7]</xref>&#x000a0;Relative contraindications are few and include pregnancy, food insecurity, and malnutrition.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-114238.r4">[4]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Common Misapplications of the Low FODMAP Diet</bold>
</p>
        <p>Several practical issues limit the safe and effective use of the low FODMAP diet in clinical practice. A significant concern is the indefinite&#x000a0;use of the elimination phase without progression to the reintroduction and personalization phases. Prolonged restriction is not evidence-based and may adversely affect gut microbiota diversity, micronutrient intake, and overall diet quality.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-114238.r7">[7]</xref>&#x000a0;Despite this, many patients follow the restrictive phase for months or years, contrary to the diet's intended short-term therapeutic design.</p>
        <p>Another common misapplication occurs when patients initiate the diet independently, relying on online or social media advice.&#x000a0;Such approaches&#x000a0;often lead to overly restrictive or incomplete implementation, confusion with gluten-free or "clean eating" patterns, and use of the diet as a first-line therapy without prior general&#x000a0;nutrition or lifestyle counseling. These approaches increase unnecessary food avoidance and make it harder to identify symptom-triggering foods.</p>
        <p>Although short-term FODMAP restriction appears generally safe and may improve diet quality when implemented under dietitian supervision, data on long-term adherence and outcomes remain limited.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-114238.r7">[7]</xref>&#x000a0;The initial restriction phase should therefore be explicitly framed as a time-limited diagnostic and therapeutic trial. In patients who do not experience meaningful symptom improvement within approximately 6 weeks, clinicians should discontinue the diet and consider&#x000a0;alternative management strategies.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Incomplete progression through the reintroduction and personalization phases is also a frequent misuse of the low FODMAP diet. Failure to systematically challenge and reintroduce FODMAP-containing foods unnecessarily restricts patients, despite evidence that up to 76% of responders can successfully liberalize their diets following structured reintroduction.&#x000a0;This misapplication undermines nutritional adequacy, dietary diversity, and long-term sustainability and may increase food-related anxiety.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-114238.r7">[7]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Nutritional and Microbiome Considerations</bold>
</p>
        <p>The restriction phase of the low FODMAP diet involves avoiding a wide range of commonly consumed foods, including wheat and rye products; certain fruits such as apricots, cherries, peaches, prunes, persimmons, and watermelon; vegetables including artichokes, beets, fennel, garlic, leeks, onions, peas, mushrooms, and some cruciferous vegetables; legumes such as lentils and chickpeas; barley; selected nuts and seeds; and foods containing polyol sweeteners.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-114238.r2">[2]</xref>&#x000a0;While this breadth of restrictions can reduce symptom burden, it also raises concerns about nutritional adequacy and long-term dietary quality if not implemented carefully.</p>
        <p>Despite these exclusions, adequate nutrient intake can often be maintained by including well-tolerated, lower-FODMAP alternatives such as tofu, select nuts and legumes, quinoa, rice, oats, lactose-free dairy products, and appropriate fruits and vegetables.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-114238.r1">[1]</xref>&#x000a0;For patients unable or unwilling to adhere to strict elimination diets, a "FODMAP-gentle" approach that targets only the highest FODMAP contributors may provide symptom relief while minimizing unnecessary dietary restriction.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-114238.r4">[4]</xref></p>
        <p>The low FODMAP diet follows a progressive, 3-phase approach that begins with a time-limited restriction phase (typically 4 to 8 weeks), proceeds to systematic reintroduction of individual FODMAP groups to identify symptom triggers, and concludes with a personalization phase that liberalizes the diet to individual tolerance while maintaining symptom control.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-114238.r5">[5]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>From a microbiome perspective, FODMAP carbohydrates act as prebiotics, supporting the growth of beneficial gut bacteria, particularly <italic toggle="yes">Bifidobacteria</italic>. Consequently, FODMAP restriction may adversely affect the gut microbiota.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-114238.r8">[8]</xref>&#x000a0;A systematic review of microbiome changes during FODMAP restriction found a consistent reduction in <italic toggle="yes">Bifidobacteria</italic> abundance across 9 studies. However, no consistent negative effects on overall microbial diversity or fecal short-chain fatty acid (SCFA) concentrations were observed.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-114238.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-114238.r9">[9]</xref>&#x000a0;These findings suggest that although microbiome changes occur, their clinical relevance remains uncertain when the restriction is time-limited and followed by reintroduction.</p>
        <p>Nutritional concerns extend beyond microbiome effects. Reducing FODMAP intake may impair intake of key micronutrients, as several high-FODMAP foods are important sources of these nutrients. For example, fructan-containing wheat products are often fortified with folic acid, B vitamins, and iron, while lactose-containing dairy products provide calcium in a highly bioavailable form.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-114238.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-114238.r10">[10]</xref>&#x000a0;Additionally, many high-FODMAP fruits, vegetables, and grains provide substantial amounts of dietary fiber. These considerations underscore the importance of avoiding unnecessary long-term restriction.</p>
        <p>The reintroduction phase plays a central role in mitigating nutritional and microbiome risks. During this phase, high-FODMAP foods are reintroduced individually, with portion sizes gradually increased over 3 days to assess tolerance.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-114238.r5">[5]</xref>&#x000a0;In clinical practice, clinicians typically use 1 representative food from each FODMAP category&#x02014;for example, milk for lactose challenges. For fructans, clinicians recommend multiple food challenges (eg, wheat, rye, onion, garlic) because differences in polymer length and fermentation characteristics influence gas production and symptom generation.</p>
        <p>Commonly challenged foods include wheat bread, onion, garlic, milk, and wheat pasta. Notably, fewer than half of patients report symptoms during any given food challenge, indicating that most challenges do not provoke symptoms.&#x000a0;This finding underscores the importance of systematic reintroduction, enabling patients to safely reintroduce tolerated foods into their diet.</p>
        <p>The final personalization phase integrates these findings into a sustainable, individualized, long-term eating pattern that maximizes dietary diversity, meets nutrient requirements, and minimizes adverse effects on the microbiome.&#x000a0;Improved understanding of personal dietary triggers enhances patient autonomy, supports long-term self-management, and reduces unnecessary dietary restriction, addressing many safety concerns associated with prolonged FODMAP avoidance.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Implementation Challenges</bold>
</p>
        <p>Successful use of the low FODMAP diet requires careful planning and interprofessional collaboration. Missteps often stem from the diet's complexity and the need to progress systematically through all 3 phases. Clinicians and dietitians must ensure that patients understand the distinction between restriction and personalization, that dietary changes are time-limited, and that nutritional adequacy is maintained. Failure to provide structured guidance may limit effectiveness and increase the risk of unintended consequences, including nutrient deficiencies and psychosocial stress related to restrictive eating.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Successful dietary therapy begins with comprehensive education.&#x000a0;Dietitian-led education is considered the gold standard for delivering low FODMAP guidance. Registered dietitians provide personalized advice on suitable food substitutions, structured guidance for the reintroduction and personalization phases, and ongoing support to reinforce patients' confidence in applying the diet in real-world settings.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-114238.r11">[11]</xref> Dietitian-led group education has demonstrated clinical effectiveness, increased capacity, and reduced costs compared with individual sessions. However, demand often exceeds the available workforce capacity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-114238.r12">[12]</xref> Alternative delivery methods, such as sessions led by other healthcare professionals, digital tools, or printed materials, may be considered to address this limitation.</p>
        <p>Patients should receive detailed information on how FODMAPs induce symptoms, helping them understand the rationale for the 3-phase protocol and the importance of gradual dietary liberalization.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-114238.r5">[5]</xref> Written materials, meal plans, and recipe ideas improve understanding and adherence, particularly when paired with verbal counseling.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-114238.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-114238.r13">[13]</xref> Education should also include guidance on tolerated foods to ensure adequate nutrient and dietary fiber intake, supporting optimal bowel habits and overall nutritional status.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-114238.s3" sec-type="Clinical Significance">
        <title>Clinical Significance</title>
        <p>
<bold>Mechanistic Rationale</bold>
</p>
        <p>FODMAPs are short-chain carbohydrates that are incompletely absorbed in the small intestine and pass into the colon, where they undergo rapid fermentation by colonic bacteria, leading to increased luminal water and gas production and, in patients with visceral hypersensitivity,&#x000a0;functional symptoms of distension, bloating, and abdominal discomfort.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-114238.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-114238.r9">[9]</xref> The following list provides examples of the various types of FODMAPs:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Oligosaccharides:</bold> Fructans found in wheat, onions, and garlic, and galacto-oligosaccharides found in beans and legumes are common sources of oligosaccharides.&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Disaccharides:</bold> Lactose is the only disaccharide considered a FODMAP and may trigger symptoms in individuals with lactase deficiency; other disaccharides, such as maltose and sucrose, are generally well absorbed and are not classified as FODMAPs.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Monosaccharides:</bold>&#x000a0;Fructose in foods such as mango, figs, and honey is the only monosaccharide considered a FODMAP, and symptoms arise specifically from excess fructose relative to glucose. In foods in which fructose exceeds glucose, such as honey and mango, fructose is poorly absorbed, thereby potentially increasing osmotic load and causing functional symptoms. The small intestine absorbs fructose by the GLUT5 and the GLUT2 transporters.&#x000a0;When fructose intake exceeds the absorptive capacity of GLUT5, often at intakes above approximately 20 g in a single meal, glucose facilitates additional fructose absorption through GLUT2. However, when fructose exceeds glucose, free fructose is poorly absorbed, which can lead to gastrointestinal symptoms.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Polyols:</bold> Sorbitol, mannitol, and synthetic polyols such as xylitol and maltitol are sugar alcohols and considered FODMAP ingredients. Several fruits, particularly stone fruits such as peaches, apricots, and plums, as well as apples and pears, contain sorbitol. Manufacturers use sorbitol as a sugar substitute in diet beverages, gum, and candy. At the same time, mushrooms and vegetables such as cauliflower naturally contain mannitol, which also appears as a sweetener in gum, candy, reduced-sugar foods, and chewable medications.&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>&#x000a0;<bold>Evidence Base</bold></p>
        <p>Randomized controlled trials demonstrate that low FODMAP diets improve abdominal bloating, pain, and stool consistency in patients with IBS.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-114238.r2">[2]</xref>&#x000a0;Comparative studies suggest benefits over traditional IBS dietary advice.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-114238.r5">[5]</xref>&#x000a0;Most trials emphasize the restriction phase and are limited by short duration and symptom-based endpoints. While symptom improvement is common, the diet does not modify the underlying disease process, and patient responses remain highly variable.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-114238.r7">[7]</xref>&#x000a0;Data from a randomized controlled trial at the University of Michigan indicate that a low FODMAP diet improves quality of life, psychosocial outcomes, and work performance in adults with IBS-D.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-114238.r14">[14]</xref>&#x000a0;A low FODMAP diet may also reduce functional gastrointestinal symptoms in selected patients with IBD in remission and IBS-like symptoms,&#x000a0;symptomatic uncomplicated diverticular disease, and other conditions characterized by IBS-like symptoms, although evidence outside IBS remains limited.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-114238.r15">[15]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-114238.r16">[16]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-114238.r17">[17]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-114238.r18">[18]</xref></p>
        <p>The following list provides an overview of international guidelines and consensus positions:&#x000a0;</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>American College of Gastroenterology:</bold> Recommends a low FODMAP diet as second-line therapy after standard dietary and lifestyle interventions.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-114238.r2">[2]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>American Gastroenterological Association</bold>: Emphasizes dietitian-led, structured implementation and time-limited restriction with systematic reintroduction.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-114238.r7">[7]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>United Kingdom National Institute for Health and Care Excellence:</bold> Recommends selective use in patients with persistent IBS symptoms under&#x000a0;professional supervision.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-114238.r2">[2]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Across organizations, guidelines advise against universal restriction and emphasize structured reintroduction to restore dietary variety and protect nutritional adequacy and the gut microbiome.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-114238.r5">[5]</xref>&#x000a0;This approach promotes dietary diversity, restores prebiotic exposure, and empowers patients to self-manage symptoms while avoiding unnecessary nutritional restriction.</p>
        <p>&#x000a0;<bold>Strengths and Limitations of Clinical Impact</bold></p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Strengths:</bold> Most patients experience symptom relief within weeks. Structured elimination of foods allows identification of personal triggers.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-114238.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-114238.r7">[7]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Limitations:</bold>&#x000a0;Prolonged FODMAP restriction may lead to reduced intake of calcium, B vitamins, iron, dietary fiber, and prebiotic-rich foods, increasing the risk of nutritional inadequacy and reductions in beneficial gut bacteria, particularly <italic toggle="yes">Bifidobacterium</italic>.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-114238.r5">[5]</xref>&#x000a0;Inadequate long-term fiber intake increases cardiometabolic and colorectal disease risk, micronutrient deficiencies, reduces the abundance of key commensal bacteria (including&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Bifidobacterium</italic>&#x000a0;and&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Faecalibacterium prausnitzii</italic>), lowers beneficial short-chain fatty acid production, and worsens constipation.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-114238.s4" sec-type="Nursing, Allied Health, and Interprofessional Team Interventions">
        <title>Nursing, Allied Health, and Interprofessional Team Interventions</title>
        <p>The low FODMAP diet is a structured, evidence-based dietary intervention used primarily to manage symptoms of IBS and related functional gastrointestinal disorders. It works by reducing the intake of poorly absorbed, fermentable carbohydrates that increase intestinal water content and gas production, thereby triggering symptoms such as bloating, abdominal pain, and altered bowel habits in susceptible individuals. The diet follows a 3-phase framework&#x02014;short-term restriction, systematic reintroduction, and personalization&#x02014;designed to identify individual triggers while ultimately liberalizing the diet to maintain nutritional adequacy and dietary variety. When implemented appropriately, the low FODMAP diet can significantly improve symptoms and quality of life; however, because of its restrictive and diagnostically complex nature, it requires careful guidance to avoid unnecessary long-term limitation, nutritional compromise, or adverse effects on the gut microbiota.</p>
        <p>Effective use of the low FODMAP diet depends on coordinated interprofessional care that integrates clinical expertise, patient education, and ongoing monitoring. Physicians and advanced practice clinicians play a central role in diagnosing IBS, excluding organic disease, setting realistic expectations for symptom improvement, and coordinating referrals to dietitians. Gastroenterology specialists confirm diagnoses in complex cases and ensure that dietary therapy aligns with broader gastrointestinal management plans. Registered dietitians provide the cornerstone of care by delivering individualized, evidence-based counseling, translating FODMAP principles into practical food substitutions, guiding reintroduction and personalization, and supporting long-term dietary flexibility. Nurses reinforce education during follow-up encounters, monitor adherence and symptom response, and communicate early concerns to the care team, enhancing continuity and patient safety. Behavioral health specialists contribute by addressing food-related anxiety, screening for disordered eating, and supporting patients whose symptoms intersect with psychological stressors. Clear interprofessional communication and shared decision-making align dietary strategies with patient goals, reduce misinformation, and ensure timely adjustments. Through collaborative skills, shared strategies, and coordinated follow-up, healthcare teams enhance patient-centered care, improve symptom control and quality of life, minimize risks associated with dietary restriction, and strengthen overall team performance in managing functional gastrointestinal disorders with the low FODMAP diet.</p>
      </sec>
